sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: If as many odd numbers as we please are added together, and their multitude is even, then the sum is even.
----
proof:

Let as many odd numbers as we please, *AB, BC, CD,* and *DE,* even in multitude, be added together.

I say that the sum *AE* is even.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="250" height="60" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="IX.22 diagram" src="propIX21.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "IX.21",
        elements: [
            "A0;point;free;-1000,30;0;0",
            "A3;point;free;1000,30;0;0",
            "A;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,30,0",
            "B;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,72,0",
            "C;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,120,0",
            "D;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,194,0",
            "E;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,220,0",
            "AE;line;connect;A,E"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

Since each of the numbers *AB, BC, CD,* and *DE* is odd, if a unit is subtracted from each, then each of the remainders is even, so that the sum of them is even. But the multitude of the units is also even. Therefore the sum *AE* is also even. [!just VII.Def.7; IX.21]

Therefore, *if as many odd numbers as we please are added together, and their multitude is even, then the sum is even.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

A critical step in the proof is the claim that if 1 is subracted from an odd number, then the remainder is even. This was mentioned in @VII.Def.7, but never proved. See the Guide for that definition for details. Unless that gap is filled, this proposition, along with many that depend upon it, are unjustified.

#### Use of this proposition

This proposition is used in the next one.
